# Task: Python MCP server that delegates Copilot's reasoning to M365 Copilot

## Architecture — read this first, do not redesign it
MCP servers expose TOOLS, not models. There is no way to make M365 be
Copilot's language model. So the design is:

  - GitHub Copilot stays the hands: reads workspace, calls tools, writes
    files. Runs on the free base model, so no premium requests.
  - All real reasoning is delegated OUT via an MCP tool that drives the
    M365 Copilot Chat web UI (https://m365.cloud.microsoft/chat) with
    Playwright and returns the answer as text.
  - Copilot then applies that answer to the workspace itself.

Do not propose BYOK, a model provider, or an OpenAI-compatible shim.
Build the MCP tool server described below.

## HARD CONSTRAINTS — corporate locked-down machine
- Python only. No Node, no TypeScript.
- `pip install playwright` is fine. It does NOT download browsers.
- NEVER run `playwright install`. Not as a fallback, not in a try/except,
  not in a setup script. It will fail behind the proxy.
- Drive the ALREADY-INSTALLED Microsoft Edge:
      p.chromium.launch_persistent_context(user_data_dir, channel="msedge")
  If channel="msedge" fails, fall back to an explicit executable_path from
  an env var. NEVER fall back to downloading a browser.
- Use a COPY of my Edge profile (%LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\Edge\User Data)
  as user_data_dir, so the session is already Entra-authenticated and the
  device is already Conditional-Access trusted. No separate login flow.
- ONLY third-party dependency: playwright.
  Do NOT use the `mcp` SDK — it pulls pydantic -> pydantic-core, a
  Rust-compiled wheel that fails to install here. Hand-roll the JSON-RPC.
  Use stdlib only for everything else (asyncio, json, sys, pathlib).

